<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-13" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Applications of Vector-Valued Functions</title>
   <introduction>
    <p>
      Vector-valued functions or space curves are useful ways to analyze objects and motion in three-dimensional space.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <objectives>
    <title>Applications of Vector-Valued Functions</title>
     <introduction>

    <p>
    In this section we will look at:
    </p>

  </introduction>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Calculating arc length.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Calculating curvature.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The TNB frame
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Motion in Space
        </p>
      </li>
          </ul>
  </objectives>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-arclength">
    <title>Arclength</title>
    <p>
     In Math 152 (Calculus II) you learned howto calculate arc length for a function, <m>y=f(x)</m>. Except for a few carefully crafted examples, arc length in general is 
     difficult to calculate due the square root in the integral from the Pythagorean theorem. With vector-valued functions, we can write a more general formula for arc
     length that is easily extended to three dimensions.
    </p>
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-arclength">
      <title>Arc Length Formulas</title>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\text{Math 152 Formula} \quad\amp \int_a^b \sqrt{1+f'(x)^2}dx </mrow>
          <mrow>\text{2D Space} \quad \amp \int_a^b \sqrt{x'(t)^2+y'(t)^2}dt </mrow>
          <mrow>\text{3D Space} \quad \amp \int_a^b \sqrt{x'(t)^2+y'(t)^2+z'(t)^2}dt</mrow>
          <mrow>\text{where} \quad\amp \mathbf{r}(t)=\langle x(t),y(t),z(t)\rangle</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <example>
      <p>
        Find the arc length of <m>\langle 2(t-\sin(t)), 2(1-\cos(t))\rangle</m> for <m>0 \leq t \leq 2\pi</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Start by calculating the derivatives <m>x'(t) = 2(1-\cos(t))</m> and <m>y'(t) = 2\sin(t)</m> Then arc length is 
        <md>
          \int_0^{2\pi} \sqrt{(2(1-\cos(t)))^2+(2\sin(t))^2}dt =\\ \int_0^{2\pi} \sqrt{4-8\cos(t)+4\cos^2(t)+4\sin^2(t)}dt=\\
          \int_0^{2\pi}\sqrt{8-8\cos(t)}dt = \\\int_0^{2\pi}\sqrt{16\sin^2(t/2)}dt =\\ \int_0^{2\pi}4\sin(t/2) dt = 16 
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </example>
<example>
      <p>
        Find the arc length of <m>\langle 3t+5, 7t-6, -4t+9\rangle</m> for <m>0 \leq t \leq 5</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Start by calculating the derivatives <m>x'(t) = 3</m>, <m>y'(t) = 7</m> and <m>z'(t) = -4</m> Then arc length is 
        <md>
          \int_0^5 \sqrt{3^2+7^2+(-4)^2}dt = \int_0^5 \sqrt{74} dt = 5\sqrt{74}
        </md>
              </p>
    </example>
<p>
  Notice in this last example, that the space "curve" was just a straight line and that the arc length was just the length of the line from <m>t=0</m> to <m>t=5</m>.
  In terms of vectors, we calculate the magnitude of the linear vector. In fact we can define an arc length <em>function</em> for any space curve called the arc length 
  parametrization:
  <md>
    \text{arc length} \,\,\, = s(t) = \int_a^t ||\mathbf{r}'(u)|| du
  </md>
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Find the arc length parametrization of the circle of radius 4 defined by <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle 4\cos(t), 4\sin(t) \rangle</m>
      <md>
        s(t) = \int_0^t ||\langle -4\sin(u), 4\cos(u)\rangle|| du = \int_0^t\sqrt{16\sin^2(u)+16\cos^2(u)} du = \int_0^t 4du = 4t
      </md>
      This defines a relationship between arc length <m>s</m> and the original parameter <m>t</m>. If we take this to mean time, than one revolution of the circle 
      which takes <m>2\pi</m> radians of time to traverse would have an arc length (circumference) of <m>4\cdot2\pi = 8\pi</m>.
    </p>
  </example>
<p>
  This arc length parametrization is key to defining the next application: curvature.
</p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-curvature">
    <title>Curvature</title>
    <p>
      The <em>curvature</em> of a space curve is defined as 
      <md>\kappa = ||\mathbf{T}'(s)||</md>
      where <m>\mathbf{T}</m> is the unit tangent vector and <m>s</m> is the arc length parametrization of the curve. Alternatively arc length can be calculated using the following formulas:
      <md>
        <mrow>\text{in terms of}\,\, t\quad \amp \kappa = \frac{||\mathbf{T}'(t)||}{||\mathbf{r}'(t)||}</mrow>
        <mrow>\text{for 3D curves} \quad \amp \kappa = \frac{||\mathbf{r}'(t)\times\mathbf{r}''(t)||}{||\mathbf{r}'(t)||^3}</mrow>
        <mrow>\text{for} \,\,y = f(x) \quad \amp \kappa = \frac{|y''|}{[1+(y')^2]^{3/2}} </mrow>
      </md>
      Here are three example videos on calculating curvature.
    </p>
    <figure>
    <caption>Calculating curvature using formula 1 (11 minutes)</caption>
    <video youtube="T143lv8aKDU"/>
  </figure>
  <figure>
    <caption>Calculating curvature using formula 2 (12 minutes)</caption>
    <video youtube="NlcvU67YWpQ"/>
  </figure>
  <figure>
    <caption>Calculating curvature using formula 3 (6 minutes)</caption>
    <video youtube="2JKCwpxuKPY"/>
  </figure>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-tnb-frame">
    <title>The T-N-B Frame</title>
    <p>
      We already know about the unit tangent vector, a vector with magnitude of one that points along the tangent line to a space curve. By definition, a unit vector and
      its derivative are orthogonal. We call this orthogonal unit vector the normal vector.
      <md>\text{Unit Normal Vector}\,\,= \mathbf{N}(t) = \frac{\mathbf{T}'(t)}{||\mathbf{T}'(t)||}</md>
      We can then create a third unit vector that is orthogonal to both the unit tangent vector and the unit normal vector called the binormal vector by calculating a 
      cross product.
      <md>
        \text{Binormal Veector}\,\,=\mathbf{B}(t) = \mathbf{T}(t) \times \mathbf{N}(t)
      </md>
      Recall that order matters when you calculate the cross product. It's always <m>\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{T}\times\mathbf{N}</m> and <m>\mathbf{B} \ne \mathbf{N}\times\mathbf{T}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      These three vectors <m>\mathbf{T}</m>, <m>\mathbf{N}</m> and <m>\mathbf{B}</m> form a mini <m>xyz</m>-like axis that changes as you move along with the curve
      this is called T-N-B frame or Frenet frame. Play around with following GeoGebra demonstration by moving the slider for <m>t</m> and also trying out different functions.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>The T-N-B frame in GeoGebra</caption>
      <interactive geogebra="aq4CR5gP" width="100%" aspect="4:3"/>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Here is a longer video (43 minutes) with more details (and Lego) on the T-N-B frame.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>TNB Frames (Frenet-Serret)</caption>
      <video youtube="zZ6LZBxWf28"/>
    </figure>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-3d-motion">
    <title>Motion in Three-Dimensional Space</title>
    <p>
      For motion in space we are interest in position, velocity and acceleration. The relevant formulas are 
    </p>
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-3d-motion">
      <title>Motion in Space</title>
      <p>
        For a space curve <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle x(t), y(t),z(t)\rangle</m>
       <md>
        <mrow>\text{Velocity Vector}\quad \amp \mathbf{v}(t) = \mathbf{r}'(t)  </mrow>
        <mrow>\text{Speed}\quad \amp v(t) = ||\mathbf{v}(t)||</mrow>
        <mrow>\text{Acceleration Vector}\quad \amp \mathbf{a}(t) = \mathbf{v}'(t) = \mathbf(r)''(t) </mrow>
        <mrow>\text{Acceleration} \quad\amp \mathbf{a} = a_{\mathbf{T}}\mathbf{T} +a_{\mathbf{N}}\mathbf{N} </mrow>
       </md> 
       The last formulas is a break down of the acceleration vector in terms of the unit tangent and normal vectors. <m>a_{\mathbf{T}}=v'(t)</m> and
       <m>\kappa(t)v(t)^2 </m>. Here is a video that explains more about that.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <figure>
      <caption>Tangential and Normal components of Acceleration</caption>
      <video youtube="LQNC7klG0F8"/>
    </figure>
    <example>
      <p>
      Find <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m> given <m>\mathbf{a}(t) = \langle e^t, 2t, t+1\rangle </m>, <m>\mathbf{v}(0) = \langle 1, 0 ,1\rangle</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}(0) = \langle 2, 1,1\rangle</m> 
      Here we need to integrate twice:
      <md>
        \mathbf{v}(t)=\int \langle  e^t, 2t, t+1\rangle dt = \langle e^t+c_1, t^2+c_2, \frac{1}{2}t^2+t+c_3\rangle
      </md>
      Plugging the values from the initial velocity vector gives
      <md>
        \mathbf{v}(0) = \langle 1, 0 ,1\rangle = \langle e^0 + c_1, 0^2 + c_2, \frac{1}{2}0^2 + 0 + c_3 \rangle \to c_1 = 0, c_2 = 0, c_3 = 1
      </md>
      Integrating this velocity function gives the position function
      <md>
    \mathbf{r}(t)=\int \langle  e^t, t^2, \frac{1}{2}t^2+t+1\rangle dt = \langle e^t+c_4, \frac{1}{3}t^3+c_5, \frac{1}{6}t^3+\frac{1}{2}t^2+t+c_6\rangle
      </md>
       Plugging the values from the initial position vector gives
      <md>
        \mathbf{r}(0) = \langle 2, 1 ,1\rangle = \langle e^0 + c_3, \frac{1}{3}0^3 + c_5, \frac{1}{6}0^3 + \frac{1}{2}0^2+0 + c_6 \rangle \to c_4 = 1, c_5 = 1, c_3 = 1
      </md>
      </p>
      So the position vector function is <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle e^t + 1, \frac{1}{3}t^2 + 1, \frac{1}{6}t^3 + \frac{1}{2}t^2 + t + 1 \rangle </m>.    
      </example>
  <p>
     Another application for motion in space is projectile motion. Here is a video about that. The first 22 minutes is a derivation of the formulas and the remaining 25 minutes 
     is a baseball example. Feel free to skip to the example.   
  </p>
  <figure>
    <caption>Projectile Motion</caption>
    <video youtube="M1PhQMmqVOg"/>
  </figure>
